{% set homeowner_guide %}
<p>Quick reference for Monrovia homeowners:</p>

<p><strong>New construction owners:</strong> Your builder likely installed the cheapest gutters that met code. Check for: minimal downspouts (should have one per 30-40 feet of gutter), lightweight gauge aluminum, poor sealing at corners. Budget for replacement within 8-10 years or earlier if problems develop.</p>

<p><strong>Established home owners:</strong> If your gutters are 20+ years old, they've done their job. Time to assess: look for visible seam separation, staining from overflow, and soft spots in fascia boards that indicate hidden damage.</p>

<p><strong>The debris equation:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Heavy tree coverage = gutter guards strongly recommended</li>
<li>Without guards: professional cleaning 2-3 times per year</li>
<li>Watch for pine needles — they require guards designed for fine debris</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Seasonal timing:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>March:</strong> Post-winter inspection, pre-storm season check</li>
<li><strong>June:</strong> Mid-year assessment after spring storms</li>
<li><strong>November:</strong> Clear fall debris before winter</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>When to call us:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Visible overflow during rain</li>
<li>Gutters pulling away from house</li>
<li>Soft or spongy fascia boards</li>
